# Project Operations

The **orchestrator** (`tools/orch`, binary `orch`) is the **canonical
entry point for project operations**.

Before creating or executing an ad-hoc script or shell one-liner:

1. Inspect: `.\orch help` (repo-root launcher; or `cargo run --manifest-path tools/orch/Cargo.toml -- help`)
2. Check Actions / Workflows under `.orch/actions`, `.orch/workflows`
3. Check focused tools under `./tools/` (`kolibri_img`, `mkfs_utils`, `migration_gates`, …)
4. Prefer composing existing units over new shell

## Architecture (mandatory)

```text
tools/orch/   = generic Rust runtime (no Kolibri-specific APIs)
.orch/        = project Rhai (actions, workflows, config)
./tools/      = focused utilities (narrow I/O — not a second orchestrator)
project/      = CONFIG_DATA (e.g. project/build.toml)
```

Do **not** create a monolithic `tools/kolibri_ops` (or similar) that re-hosts
the old build/image/QEMU pipeline. Composition belongs in Rhai; tools stay focused.

## Agent automation policy

**`orch` is the only automation boundary used directly by the AI agent.**

Do not directly invoke PowerShell, cmd, bash, sh, Python, cargo, QEMU, FASM,
or other external programs from the agent shell for repository automation.

Use the repo-root launcher (from the repository root):

```text
.\orch --% $ <Rhai>
.\orch --% @action
.\orch --% workflow
```

On PowerShell, always pass `--%` before units that start with `$` or `@`.
Fall back to `cargo run --manifest-path tools/orch/Cargo.toml -- --% …`
only if the launcher is missing.

Invoke external programs from Rhai (`process::run`, …) when needed.

If a generic capability is missing: extend the orch runtime → expose to Rhai →
continue via `orch $`. Do not bypass the runtime.

## CLI model

```text
orch [GLOBAL_OPTIONS] [EXECUTION_UNITS...]

$ source      Inline Rhai (anonymous Action)
@name         Named Action (`:` is part of the name)
name          Workflow
::            Unit-argument terminator
```

```powershell
.\orch --% run:dev
.\orch --% @build:dev
.\orch --% @mkfs exfat 4M
.\orch --% @doctor
.\orch --% $ "log::info(\"ok\");"
```

On PowerShell, `--%` stops further parsing so `$` and `@…` are not expanded.
Optional PATH install: `cargo install --path tools/orch` then `orch --% …`
from any directory (repo root is still discovered automatically).

Filesystem layout: directory hierarchy maps to `:` names; `default.rhai` omits
the final component (`.orch/actions/build/default.rhai` → `@build`).

## Extension hierarchy

1. **Rhai Action** under `.orch/actions/`
2. **Workflow** under `.orch/workflows/`
3. **Focused tool** under `./tools/` invoked via `process::run`
4. **Extend orch Rust** only for generic primitives (process, FS, CWD, cancel, HTTP, …)

## Artifact paths

| Path | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `build/` | Production build artifacts |
| `dev_build/` | Disposable test images and temp artifacts |
| `images/` | Persistent FS regression images |
| `project/build.toml` | Blob/migration/image/QEMU CONFIG_DATA |

Do not use `tmp_images/` for new work. See `.cursor/rules/dev-build.mdc` —
delete unused temporary files under `dev_build/` after use; `@clean` wipes it.

## Image handling

See `.cursor/rules/image-handling.mdc` — reference `kolibrios-*.img` is immutable.
